<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Lusitanians</h1><p class="st-meta">500 BCE – 138 BCE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The fierce Iron Age people of central Portugal whose leader Viriathus defeated Roman armies for years before his murder in 139 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Lusitanians lived between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douro">Douro</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagus">Tagus</a>, in central Portugal and the neighbouring parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/extremadura">Extremadura</a>, emerging as a recognisable people in the second half of the first millennium BCE. They spoke Lusitanian, an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-european-languages">Indo-European</a> language known from a handful of inscriptions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> letters, such as those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cabeco-das-fraguas">Cabeço das Fráguas</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Lusitanian society was organised in clans and hillfort communities, with warrior bands under chosen leaders and a herding and farming economy in the uplands. Raiding into the richer south was a constant feature. They worshipped local gods such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bandua">Bandua</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/endovelicus">Endovelicus</a>, and their warriors fought in light, mobile formations that troubled Roman legions.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After raids from 155 BCE, the Roman praetor Galba massacred thousands under a false truce in 150 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viriathus">Viriathus</a>, a survivor, led resistance until he was assassinated in 139 BCE, after which resistance collapsed. Their land later became the Roman province of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/lusitania">Lusitania</a>, a name later revived as a poetic name for Portugal.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/lusitanians.jpg" alt="Lusitanians" width="320" height="312"><figcaption>Image: Ángel M. Felicísimo · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pectoral_(38920965500).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>500 BCE – 138 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Punicus, Viriathus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lusitanian polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Lusitanian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>, <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lusitani</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-319&amp;focus=lusitanians" aria-label="Lusitanians on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4477 752 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lusitanians lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4881 1072h0M4821 1043h0M4990 934h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4797 1008h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M4797 1008h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Portugal. <a href="/atlas?year=-319&amp;focus=lusitanians">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
